<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Get Cool in the Shade. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Get Cool in the Shade.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend the gist of short stories and news items. Interactive: Contribute actively to group projects and discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend the main idea from short announcements about conserving energy (e.g., "Turn off the lights and conserve energy."). Can actively follow and contribute to a group project on community energy conservation (e.g., "We can reduce energy consumption."). Can follow oral instructions to save energy (e.g., "Use natural light.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures related to the theme: "shade," "light," "morning," "night," "water," "energy." The teacher says each word clearly and asks students to listen and point to the correct picture. "Listen: 'shade'. Point to the shade!" "Listen: 'morning'. Point to morning!" This activates prior knowledge and introduces core vocabulary receptively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of key vocabulary and sounds. The teacher says "shade" several times, emphasizing the /sh/ sound, and then "turn off," emphasizing the /t/ sound. Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also introduces simple imperatives like "Turn off the lights!" and "Let's save energy!" with clear gestures, modeling the suggested actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and basic concepts using visuals and simple questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of someone turning off a light switch) "Is she making the room bright or dark?" (Expected answer: Dark!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of trees) "Do trees give us shade or light?" (Expected answer: Shade!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio announcement for the school about "Energy Saving Tips for a Cool School Day." The audio includes simple sentences using imperatives and core vocabulary. (e.g., "Good morning, students! Let's save energy. Turn off the lights when you leave the classroom. Use natural light in the morning. At night, remember to switch off all electricity. Conserve water! It's easier than you think!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will develop their receptive listening skills by comprehending the main ideas and specific details from short announcements related to energy conservation in a familiar context (school announcements).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and answer a general question: "What is this announcement about? A) Saving water. B) Saving energy. C) Playing games." (Expected answer: B)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and mark "True" or "False" for statements.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Turn off the lights when you leave. (True/False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Use electricity all day. (True/False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Conserve water. (True/False)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on listening for imperatives (e.g., "Turn off the lights," "Let's save energy"), key vocabulary (shade, light, water, energy, turn off, save, conserve, electricity), and identifying specific actions. Students will also be exposed to comparative "easier."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Fill-in-the-Blanks (Controlled practice):</b> The teacher plays a slightly modified version of the announcement or new short sentences. Students have a worksheet with sentences from the audio and blanks for target vocabulary words. E.g., "_____ off the lights." "Use natural _____." "Let's _____ energy." Students listen and write the missing words. (Focus: turn, light, save).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Match (Semi-controlled substitution drill/discrimination):</b> The teacher says a sentence with an imperative. Students listen and match it to one of two pictures: one showing the correct action (e.g., "Turn off the faucet") and another showing the opposite (e.g., "Water running from the faucet"). This reinforces comprehension of actions. E.g., Teacher says: "Conserve water." Students point to the picture of a closed faucet.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Act (Pair interaction/peer check):</b> In pairs, students take turns listening to the teacher's oral instructions (imperatives) and performing a simple action. Partner A listens and acts, Partner B observes and gives a thumbs up/down based on the teacher's action. E.g., Teacher says "Switch off the light." (Students pretend to switch off a light). "Use natural light." (Students pretend to open curtains). This promotes active listening and non-verbal response.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Project: "Our Energy Saving Plan." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher gives each group a large paper divided into "Morning," "Noon," and "Night." The teacher orally provides a series of instructions and suggestions. Students must listen carefully to decide where to draw or write a simple action based on the time of day. They listen to their group members' ideas and contributions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher gives oral instructions/suggestions one by one (e.g., "In the morning, use natural light." "Let's turn off the lights at noon." "At night, remember to switch off all electricity. This is the most important thing for energy!" "Saving water is easier than wasting it." "Recycle your paper, it helps the environment.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and then discuss in their groups where to represent the action (draw or write a key phrase) on their "Morning, Noon, Night" paper. (e.g., for "Use natural light," they draw a sun in the "Morning" section).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to each other's suggestions and agree on placements. They may use phrases like "I prefer putting that in the morning because..." or "Let's draw a light bulb with a cross for 'Night'."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups present their "Our Energy Saving Plan" by pointing to a section and saying the action they drew (e.g., "In the morning, use natural light.").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group project (Stage 4) and an individual exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Observation:</b> During the group work, the teacher circulates and observes students' ability to listen to instructions, contribute ideas, and use target vocabulary/imperatives in their group discussions (e.g., "Turn off the water," "Let's save electricity").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short individual task before leaving. The teacher says 2-3 short, simple sentences (e.g., "Turn off the lights." "Save water." "Use solar power!") and students draw a quick picture for each sentence or write one word that represents the action they heard.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of oral instructions (drawing/writing correct actions on exit ticket).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in group activity (active listening and following instructions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identification of key vocabulary (e.g., drawing "light" for "lights," "faucet" for "water").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new word about saving energy you learned today."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one way YOU can help save energy at home?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the energy tips was easy for you today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was a little bit difficult to understand, and how can you try to listen better next time?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Today's listening activities helped students grasp basic energy conservation concepts. The visual aids and repetitive modeling of vocabulary, especially 'turn off' and 'save energy,' were effective at A1.3. The 'Listen and Act' activity was great for kinesthetic learners and reinforced comprehension. Some students struggled with differentiating between 'morning' and 'night' instructions without visual cues during the group task, so more explicit time-of-day differentiation may be needed for future listening tasks. I'll need to reinforce the /sh/ and /t/ sounds with a specific chant next time. Overall, the group project encouraged listening and collaboration. I need to ensure that the comparatives ('easier than') and superlatives ('most important') are simplified and consistently contextualized in future listening input for better receptive understanding at this level."</p>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text or illustrated story that uses the same vocabulary and imperatives about energy conservation. This will help them solidify their understanding of the words they heard today by seeing them in written form and relating them to familiar conservation actions."</p>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>